topic classification model.

31
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.100
So the specific name of the model

32
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.700
that we're using is BART,
which was created by Meta Labs.

33
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.700
And how it really works under the hood
is actually pretty similar

34
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.300
to the natural language
inference or NLI model

35
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.300
that we've seen in our previous lesson
on hallucination,

36
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.800
except that instead of passing
in a hypothesis and a premise directly,

37
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.433
we end up using this specific
formulation of NLI,

38
00:02:06.733 --> 00:02:10.600
where our premise is our input text,
and the hypothesis is that

39
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:15.600
the text is about specific topics,
and if entailed or contradictory,

40
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.233
we end up getting information on
how likely it is

41
00:02:18.233 --> 00:02:21.600
that those topics
belong to the specific text.

42
00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:23.600
So let's see this topic

43
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.400
classification model in action and use it
to create our zero shot classifier.

44
00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:32.000
So same as before,
we are actually going to use HuggingFace

45
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.033
and HuggingFace pipelines
to create a zero shot classification

46
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.000
using the Facebook/BART-
Large-mnli model.

47
00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.100
And the hypothesis template
which we'd seen in the slide as well, is

48
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.700
this sentence above contains
discussions of the following topics

49
00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:49.600
where all of the topics we care about
will go in here.

50
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.100
Let's see how
well this classifier performs on a toy

51
00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.600
example, where the sentence is "Chick-
fil-A is closed on Sundays",

52
00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:00.100
and the potential topics we send to this
model are food, business and politics.

53
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:01.800
Perfect.

54
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:05.900
So we can see that for "Chick-
fil-A is closed on Sundays',

55
00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:10.500
food, business and politics
each come with these likelihood score.

56
00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.600
So food is like 0.672. Business.

57
00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:14.533
It is about business.

58
00:03:14.533 --> 00:03:19.300
So 0.179 and then politics 0.027.
Which is pretty close

59
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.900
to how we'd expect the sentence
to perform on specific topics.

60
00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:26.400
And this classifier, this zero shot topic

61
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.533
classifier forms
the core of our topic guardrails.

62
00:03:30.533 --> 00:03:35.400
So before we get too far
